{
  "statement_prompt": "Extract semantic facts, type declarations, inheritance relationships, rules, and negations.\n\nThe output is not PLN text. Return LangExtract objects with these extraction classes:\n- fact: a predicate with one or more arguments. Attributes: predicate, plus either arguments or subject/object.\n- type_decl: declares that an entity belongs to a type. Attributes: entity, type.\n- property: assigns a property value to an entity. Attributes: entity, property, value.\n- inheritance: subtype or kind-of relationship. Attributes: child, parent.\n- rule: a conditional implication. Attributes: head_predicate, head_args, body.\n- negation: explicit negation of a unary or binary fact. Attributes: predicate, plus either arguments or subject/object.\n\nRules:\n- Use lowercase hyphen-separated predicate names, such as eats or can-fly.\n- Entity and type names may preserve source casing.\n- Hyphens and digits inside source identifiers are preserved, such as Object-77 and Rack-01.\n- Variables in rules must start with $, such as $x.\n- rule.head_args must be a space-separated argument string.\n- rule.body must be a valid S-expression using the same predicate names.\n- Use (and ...) for multi-premise rule bodies.\n- Definitional sentences become rules with a single-atom body.\n- Emit each causal/transitive link as a separate rule.\n- Use exact source spans for extraction_text; do not paraphrase.\n- For binary predicates (e.g., eats, consumes, owns, teaches), include both subject and object when the object is present in the text.\n- Do not bake objects into predicate names when an object is present; keep the object in subject/object or arguments.\n- If a sentence adds an adverb like \"frequently\", keep the object and encode the adverb in the predicate name only if needed (e.g., eats-frequently with subject+object).\n- Portion-size phrases (e.g., \"large portions\", \"small portions\") should become facts like eats-large-portions or eats-small-portions.\n- Portion-size facts can be unary when they describe the eater, unless the object is essential for clarity.\n- Ensure every dependent clause and comma-separated phrase (e.g., \", often in large portions,\") emits its own distinct fact. Do not skip details.\n- IMPORTANT: When a proper name (capitalized word that is not at sentence start) appears as an actor in facts or rules, emit a type_decl extraction declaring that entity as a 'person' unless context clearly indicates otherwise (e.g., company names, place names). This helps the reasoner understand entity types.",
